        return q('Predict a relay limit cycle', `A relay with hysteresis switches between ±${M} and drives the plant G(s) = ${K}/[s(s+1)(s+2)] in a unity negative-feedback loop with zero reference. The relay changes state when its input crosses ±h, with h = ${h}. Its describing function is N(A) = (4M/πA)[√(1 − (h/A)²) − jh/A] for A ≥ h. Predict the limit cycle from G(jω) = −1/N(A). First show that the −1/N locus is a horizontal line. Then find the oscillation frequency, period and amplitude at the relay input. Compare with an ideal relay (h = 0). Assume the plant filters harmonics well enough for the first-harmonic approximation to hold.`, [`G(s) = ${K}/[s(s+1)(s+2)]`, `Relay output ±${M}`, `Hysteresis h = ${h}`, 'Unity negative feedback, r = 0'], [p('Oscillation frequency', w, 'rad/s'), p('Period', 2 * Math.PI / w, 's'), p('Re G(jω) at the intersection', re, ''), p('Amplitude at the relay input', A, ''), p('Ideal-relay amplitude (h = 0)', A0, ''), p('Frequency reduction caused by hysteresis', shift, '%')], ['Compute −1/N(A) = −(π/4M)[√(A² − h²) + jh]. Its imaginary part does not depend on A.', 'Solve Im G(jω) = −πh/(4M) for ω between 0 and √2 rad/s. G(jω) = K/[−3ω² + jω(2 − ω²)].', 'Then match real parts: √(A² − h²) = −(4M/π)Re G(jω). For h = 0 the intersection is at ω = √2, where G = −K/6.'], [`−1/N(A) lies on the line Im = −πh/(4M) = ${fmt(target)}.`, `Im G(jω) = ${fmt(target)} at ω = ${fmt(w)} rad/s (period ${fmt(2 * Math.PI / w)} s).`, `There Re G = ${fmt(re)}, so A = √[(4M·Re G/π)² + h²] = ${fmt(A)}.`, `Ideal relay: ω₀ = √2 rad/s and A₀ = 4MK/(6π) = ${fmt(A0)}.`, `Hysteresis lowers the frequency by ${fmt(shift)}%. As A increases, the locus moves left, out of the region enclosed by G(jω). By the Loeb criterion the limit cycle is therefore stable.`], 'Describing functions turn a nonlinear oscillation into a graphical intersection. Hysteresis adds phase lag, which lowers the oscillation frequency.', hardFeedbackDiagram(`Relay ±${M}, hysteresis ${h}`, `${K}/[s(s+1)(s+2)]`), 'Describing functions; Nyquist plots; limit cycles; nonlinear root finding.');
