        return q('Analyse a CRC as a polynomial code', `A link appends an 8-bit CRC with generator g(x) = ${name} (0x${poly.toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(2, '0')}). The CRC uses initial value 0, no reflection and no final XOR. The message bytes are ${hex}, sent most-significant bit first, followed by the CRC byte. (a) Compute the CRC. (b) If only the first transmitted bit of the 24-bit codeword flips, find the receiver’s syndrome x²³ mod g(x) as an 8-bit value. (c) Find the order e of x modulo g(x), the smallest e > 0 with x^e ≡ 1. (d) Does the code detect every odd-weight error? Enter 1 for yes and 0 for no. (e) Count the double-bit error patterns in a 256-bit codeword that go undetected.`, [`g(x) = ${name}`, `Message = ${hex}`, 'Init 0x00 · no reflection · no XOR-out', 'Codeword = 16 message bits + 8 CRC bits'], [p('CRC value (decimal, 0–255)', crc, '', 0), p('Syndrome of a first-bit error (decimal)', syn, '', 0), p('Order e of x modulo g(x)', e, '', 0), p('Detects all odd-weight errors? (1/0)', odd, '', 0), p('Undetected double-bit patterns in 256 bits', pairs, 'patterns', 0)], ['The CRC is M(x)·x⁸ mod g(x) in GF(2). Shift-register form: XOR each byte into the register, then perform eight shift-and-conditionally-XOR steps.', 'A single error at codeword position k has syndrome x^k mod g(x). The first transmitted bit has k = 23. Repeated multiplication by x is the same shift step.', 'Two errors k₁ < k₂ go undetected exactly when g divides x^(k₂−k₁) + 1, that is when e divides k₂ − k₁. g detects all odd-weight errors when (x + 1) divides it, i.e. when g has an even number of terms.'], [`CRC = 0x${crc.toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(2, '0')} = ${crc}.`, `x²³ mod g = 0x${syn.toString(16).toUpperCase().padStart(2, '0')} = ${syn}.`, `Order e = ${e}. Every codeword of length ≤ ${e} detects all double-bit errors.`, `g has ${ones} terms, so x + 1 ${odd ? 'divides' : 'does not divide'} it. Odd-weight detection: ${odd}.`, `Undetected double-bit patterns in 256 bits = Σ(256 − ke) over ke < 256 = ${pairs}.`], 'A CRC is polynomial arithmetic over GF(2). Its error-detecting power follows from the algebra of the generator, not from luck.', hardBlock(`CRC-8 · g(x) = ${name}`, `Message ${hex} → append CRC → 24-bit codeword`), 'Polynomial arithmetic in GF(2); linear feedback shift registers; cyclic codes; multiplicative order.');
